<p>When a <strong>Japanese Wagyu certificate</strong> is shown beside a piece of beef, it is tempting to focus on the animal photograph, a seal or a prominent grade such as A5 and assume that the document answers every question at once. In reality, a certificate, carcass record or traceability document may combine several different types of information: individual identification, country and region of origin, carcass grade, BMS and, where applicable, details of a regional beef brand.</p>
<p>The most useful way to read that documentation is <strong>field by field</strong>. Each item proves something specific and should not be used to prove something it does not establish. A5 is a grade; BMS describes marbling; an individual ID connects the animal to Japan’s traceability system; and a brand such as Kobe has additional rules of its own. This guide is designed for interpreting those details when you already have a document or product record in front of you. If your main question is whether an offer is genuine before buying, see our guide to <a href="/en/how-to-tell-authentic-japanese-wagyu-certification-traceability/">identifying authentic Japanese Wagyu</a>.</p>
<h2>First: there is no single universal “Wagyu certificate”</h2>
<p>Not every Japanese Wagyu product reaches the customer with an identical sheet of paper. Documentation can include carcass records, supplier information, export labels, individual identification records and certificates specific to particular regional brands. A large primal may also be divided and repacked before reaching a retail customer.</p>
<p>For that reason, the design of the paper is not the important part. The useful questions are <strong>what type of document you are looking at, which product it represents and how the seller connects it to the beef being sold</strong>. A specialist retailer should be able to explain that connection without relying only on phrases such as “genuine” or “premium A5.”</p>
<h2>1. Individual identification number: the animal reference</h2>
<p>Japan operates an individual cattle identification system. Each animal has an identifier that can be linked to information recorded through its life and through the production chain. In Japanese beef documentation, that identifier is one of the key elements for understanding traceability.</p>
<p>When a number appears, do not automatically assume it is the individual cattle ID. A commercial lot number, warehouse reference or retailer code can appear on the same paperwork. The practical question is: <strong>does this number identify the animal, the carcass, a lot or the repacked retail product?</strong></p>
<h2>2. Origin: country, prefecture and brand are different fields</h2>
<p>Origin is the next layer. Beef described specifically as Japanese Wagyu should be linked to production in Japan. The documentation may then identify a prefecture, production area or regional brand.</p>
<p>These terms should be read separately. Hyogo is a prefecture; Kobe Beef is a brand with specific requirements. Miyazaki and Kagoshima can appear as geographic and commercial references, but a place name does not replace carcass grading or individual identification.</p>
<p>Clear documentation allows you to distinguish <strong>geographic provenance</strong> from a controlled programme or brand with its own rules.</p>
<h2>3. Breed or bloodline: what it tells you</h2>
<p>Documentation may state the breed, particularly Japanese Black or Kuroge Washu, which accounts for most Wagyu production in Japan. Some programmes may also reference a bloodline or population such as Tajima in the context of Hyogo.</p>
<p>This information describes the animal, but it does not replace the quality grade. Japanese Black does not automatically mean A5, just as A5 does not automatically make beef Kobe.</p>
<h2>4. A5, A4 and other combinations: how to read the grade</h2>
<p>Japanese carcass grading combines a letter and a number. A, B or C describes estimated yield, while 1 to 5 represents quality grade. A5 is therefore not a breed, an origin or a certificate of authenticity: it is a <strong>carcass classification</strong>.</p>
<p>If the document states A5, the correct reading is that the carcass achieved yield grade A and quality grade 5 under the Japanese system. For a detailed explanation, see <a href="/en/japanese-wagyu-a5-what-it-is-what-it-means/">what Japanese Wagyu A5 means</a>.</p>
<p>This distinction matters because professional documentation keeps grade separate from other commercial claims. Japanese Wagyu does not all have to be A5, and a regional brand may impose additional conditions beyond the grade.</p>
<h2>5. BMS: the specific marbling field</h2>
<p>The Beef Marbling Standard uses a scale from 1 to 12 to describe marbling. It is more specific than simply reading A5 because the marbling criterion within quality grade 5 covers more than one BMS level.</p>
<p>Two A5 carcasses can therefore have different marbling scores. BMS helps explain that difference, but <strong>it does not establish country of origin or regional brand</strong>. A high BMS number without traceability does not prove that the beef came from Japan.</p>
<h2>6. Regional brand: what to verify beyond the name</h2>
<p>When a certificate or product record uses a name such as Kobe, check whether it is simply a geographic reference or beef that actually satisfies the brand’s rules. Kobe Beef, for example, is subject to requirements involving Tajima cattle, production within the framework defined by its association, carcass criteria and authorised distribution.</p>
<p>A certificate linked to a regional brand can therefore provide information beyond a generic Japanese Wagyu record. Our <a href="/en/wagyu-vs-kobe-differences/">Wagyu vs Kobe</a> guide explains why those labels should not be treated as synonyms.</p>
<h2>7. Cut, weight and lot: connecting the document to your portion</h2>
<p>A certificate may refer to an animal or full carcass while the consumer buys 100, 200 or 300 grams of one cut. Between those two points, the beef may be broken down, portioned and distributed through several commercial stages.</p>
<p>The retailer should therefore be able to connect the retail item to its source documentation through lot references, labels or internal traceability records. You may not always receive the original carcass document physically in the box, but there should be a verifiable relationship between your portion and the primal or carcass from which it came.</p>
<h2>8. Seals and logos: useful, but never enough on their own</h2>
<p>The Universal Wagyu Mark and marks used by particular organisations can help identify programmes and products. A photographed or reproduced logo on a website, however, cannot replace information that identifies the beef itself.</p>
<p>The strongest signal is <strong>consistency across all the fields</strong>: country, prefecture, breed where declared, identifier, grade, BMS, regional brand and the retail item should all tell the same story. If one element conflicts with the others, ask for clarification.</p>
<h2>How to read a certificate in order: a practical check</h2>
<p>When you have a certificate or an image of the paperwork in front of you, use this sequence:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Identify the document type:</strong> regional certificate, carcass record, label, registry information or commercial paperwork.</li>
<li><strong>Locate the identifier:</strong> distinguish individual cattle ID, carcass number, lot and retail reference.</li>
<li><strong>Check origin:</strong> country and, where stated, prefecture or production area.</li>
<li><strong>Separate breed from brand:</strong> Japanese Black, Tajima, Kobe and other terms do not mean the same thing.</li>
<li><strong>Read the grade:</strong> A/B/C and 1–5 describe yield and quality.</li>
<li><strong>Look for BMS where provided:</strong> treat it as marbling information, not proof of origin.</li>
<li><strong>Connect the document to your portion:</strong> cut, weight, lot and retailer records should preserve traceability.</li>
</ol>
<p>This sequence avoids the common mistake of starting with the biggest number on the page and ignoring everything else.</p>
<h2>What does an inconsistency mean?</h2>
<p>An inconsistency does not automatically prove fraud because repacking, translation and different commercial formats can sometimes explain apparent differences. It does, however, justify a question. A listing that claims Kobe without explaining the brand, uses A5+ as if it were an official grade above A5, or gives an ambiguous origin deserves additional verification.</p>
<p>Premium documentation should reduce uncertainty rather than create it. The more specific and valuable the claim — Japanese origin, a particular regional brand or a high carcass grade — the easier it should be to understand which field supports it.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Does every Japanese Wagyu product have the same certificate?</h3>
<p>No. Document formats vary by brand, operator, distribution chain and retail format. What matters is that traceability exists and that the seller can connect the retail item to the relevant documentation.</p>
<h3>Is A5 a certificate of origin?</h3>
<p>No. A5 combines yield grade A and quality grade 5 within the carcass grading system. Origin is supported by separate identification and traceability information.</p>
<h3>Does BMS 12 prove the beef is Japanese Wagyu?</h3>
<p>No. BMS describes marbling. A high marbling score does not replace identification and origin records.</p>
<h3>Do I need to receive the original certificate?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily when buying a small retail portion. The essential point is that a documentary chain can connect that portion to the animal, carcass, lot or original primal according to the system being used.</p>
<h2>The conclusion</h2>
<p><strong>Reading a Japanese Wagyu certificate means separating what each field actually proves.</strong> The individual number supports traceability; origin describes provenance; breed identifies the cattle; A4 or A5 expresses carcass grade; BMS specifies marbling; and a regional brand can add its own conditions.</p>
<p>When those elements are consistent, the document stops being a decorative extra and becomes a useful tool for understanding exactly what beef you are buying.</p>
